Let $w = e^{i \pi / 12}$. Then
\begin{align*}
w^3 &= \left(e^{i \pi / 12}\right)^3 = e^{i \pi / 4} \\
w^{-3} &= \left(e^{i \pi / 12}\right)^{-3} = e^{-i \pi / 4}
\end{align*}
Using Euler's formula, $e^{i \theta} = \cos \theta + i \sin \theta$,
\begin{align*}
w^3 &= e^{i \pi / 4} = \cos \frac{\pi}{4} + i \sin \frac{\pi}{4} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}} \\
w^{-3} &= e^{-i \pi / 4} = \cos \left(- \frac{\pi}{4}\right) + i \sin \left(- \frac{\pi}{4}\right) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} - \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{align*}
So
\begin{align*}
w^3 + w^{-3} &= \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} - \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\right) \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} - \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}} \\
&= \frac{2}{\sqrt{2}} \\
&= \sqrt{2}
\end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*}
\left(w^3 + w^{-3}\right)^2 &= \left(\sqrt{2}\right)^2 \\
&= \boxed{2}

