\[ c = \sqrt{a^2-b^2} = \sqrt{4^2-3^2} = \sqrt{16-9} = \sqrt{7} \]

Thus the coordinates of the foci are $(0, \pm\sqrt{7})$.

Now let us translate the ellipse to the new center $(h, k)$. This means we simply need to add $(h,k)$ to the old coordinates of the foci, so the new coordinates of the foci are $(h, k\pm\sqrt{7})$.

Now we need to rotate these new coordinates by an angle $\alpha$ around the center $(h,k)$. To do this, we can use the rotation formulas:
\begin{align*}
x' &= x\cos(\alpha) - y\sin(\alpha) \\
y' &= x\sin(\alpha) + y\cos(\alpha)
\end{align*}
We will apply these rotation formulas to the points $(h, k+\sqrt{7})$ and $(h, k-\sqrt{7})$. For the first point, we have:
\begin{align*}
x' &= h\cos(\alpha) - (k+\sqrt{7})\sin(\alpha) \\
y' &= h\sin(\alpha) + (k+\sqrt{7})\cos(\alpha)
\end{align*}
And for the second point, we have:
\begin{align*}
x' &= h\cos(\alpha) - (k-\sqrt{7})\sin(\alpha) \\
y' &= h\sin(\alpha) + (k-\sqrt{7})\cos(\alpha)
\end{align*}
Simplifying, the new coordinates of the foci are:
\begin{align*}
(h - (k+\sqrt{7})\sin(\alpha), k\cos(\alpha)+h\sin(\alpha)+\sqrt{7}\cos(\alpha)) \text{ and } \\
(h - (k-\sqrt{7})\sin(\alpha), k\cos(\alpha)+h\sin(\alpha)-\sqrt{7}\cos(\alpha))
\end{align*}
Using some basic algebra to factor, we get that the new coordinates of the foci are:
\begin{align*}
(h - k\sin(\alpha)-\sqrt{7}\sin(\alpha), h\sin(\alpha)+k\cos(\alpha)+\sqrt{7}\cos(\alpha)) \text{ and } \\
(h - k\sin(\alpha)+\sqrt{7}\sin(\alpha), h\sin(\alpha)+k\cos(\alpha)-\sqrt{7}\cos(\alpha))
\end{align*}
Therefore, the new coordinates of the foci are $\boxed{(h-k\sin(\alpha)\pm\sqrt{7}\sin(\alpha),k\cos(\alpha)+h\sin(\alpha)\pm\sqrt{7}\cos(\alpha))}

